CHAPTER XIV PROHIBITIONS

Article 54

Every person is prohibited from:
a. treating Women and Children in a discriminatory manner that results in Women and Children experiencing losses, both material and moral, thereby hindering their social function;
b. treating persons with disabilities in a discriminatory manner;
c. placing, allowing, involving, ordering the involvement of Children in situations of maltreatment and neglect;
d. placing, allowing, committing, ordering to commit or participating in committing Violence against Women and Children;
e. committing Violence or threat of Violence to force Women and Children to have sexual intercourse with him or her or with another person;
f. committing Violence or threat of violence, forcing, committing fraud, committing a series of lies, or persuading Women and Children to commit or allow obscene acts to be committed;
g. placing, allowing, committing, ordering to commit or participating in committing kidnapping, sale and/or trafficking of Women and Children;
h. obstructing Women and Children from enjoying their own culture, acknowledging and practicing their religious teachings and/or using their own language without neglecting access to Community and cultural development;
i. placing, allowing, committing, ordering to commit or participating in committing economic and/or sexual exploitation of Women and Children;
j. intentionally placing, allowing, involving Women and Children in the abuse, as well as production and distribution of narcotics and/or psychotropic substances; and/or
k. intentionally placing, allowing, involving, ordering the involvement of Women and Children in the abuse, as well as production and distribution of alcohol and other addictive substances.

Part Three Handling

Article 23

1. The targets of Child handling include:
a. Children outside the care of parents;
b. Children in disaster emergency situations;
c. Children in conflict with the law;
d. Child victims of physical, mental and/or sexual Violence;
e. Children subjected to maltreatment and neglect;
f. Child victims of economic exploitation;
g. Children who are victims of the crime of trafficking in persons;
h. Children who are victims of gambling, narcotics abuse, alcohol, psychotropic substances and other addictive substances (Narcotics); and
i. Children in situations where the nature and condition of the workplace may endanger the health, safety, and morals of the Child.
2. Handling as referred to in paragraph (1) must be carried out immediately including:
a. identifying and receiving complaints/reports;
b. rescue actions;
c. placement of Children in temporary shelters;
d. rehabilitation including:
1\. health recovery services;
2\. psychosocial recovery services; and
3\. legal assistance/expert witness assistance.
e. social reintegration in the form of post-rehabilitation service support.

CHAPTER XII

COMMUNITY PARTICIPATION

Article 51

1. Regional Government has the obligation to strengthen Community participation in the development of Women's Empowerment and Child Protection.
2. Community participation as referred to in paragraph (1) may be carried out in the form of:
a. socialization;
b. advocacy;
c. counseling;
d. guidance;
e. supervision;
f. facilitation;
g. services;
h. studies;
i. training;
j. capital; and
k. provision of infrastructure and facilities.
3. Strengthening of Community participation as referred to in paragraph (1) may be carried out through:
a. strengthening cooperation;
b. strengthening institutional capacity for prevention and services from the Community, Islamic boarding schools and the business sector; and
c. financing support.

REGIONAL REGULATION OF TANAH BUMBU REGENCY

NUMBER 12 OF 2023

CONCERNING

IMPLEMENTATION OF WOMEN'S EMPOWERMENT AND CHILD PROTECTION

BY THE GRACE OF ALMIGHTY GOD

REGENT OF TANAH BUMBU,

Considering:

a. that Women and Children have the right to receive protection from torture, threats, pressure, and have the right to receive equal treatment and opportunities to obtain justice and welfare in accordance with the mandate of the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia;
b. that Women's Empowerment and protecting the rights of Children are obligations of the Regional Government and the Community in the Region;
c. that based on the provisions of Article 12 paragraph (2) letter b and letter H Division of Government Affairs in the Field of Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Appendix to Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government as has been amended several times most recently by Law Number 6 of 2023 concerning Stipulation of Government Regulation in Lieu of Law Number 2 of 2022 concerning Job Creation into Law, the Regional Government has the authority to implement Women's Empowerment and Child Protection in the Region;
d. that based on the considerations as referred to in letter a, letter b, and letter c, it is necessary to establish a Regional Regulation concerning Women's Empowerment and Child Protection;

In View of:

1. Law Number 4 of 1979 concerning Child Welfare (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1979 Number 32, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3143);
2. Law Number 7 of 1984 concerning Ratification of the Convention on the Elimination of All Forms of Discrimination Against Women (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1984 Number 29, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3277);
3. Law Number 39 of 1999 concerning Human Rights (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1999 Number 165, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3886);
4. Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2002 Number 102, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4235) as has been amended several times most recently by Law Number 1 of 2023 concerning the Criminal Code (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2023 Number 1, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 6842);
5. Law Number 13 of 2003 concerning Manpower (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2003 Number 39, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4279) as has been amended several times most recently by Law Number 6 of 2023 concerning Stipulation of Government Regulation in Lieu of Law Number 2 of 2022 concerning Job Creation into Law (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2023 Number 41, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 6856);
6. Law Number 6 of 2014 concerning Villages (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 7, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5495) as has been amended several times most recently by Law Number 6 of 2023 concerning Stipulation of Government Regulation in Lieu of Law Number 2 of 2022 concerning Job Creation into Law (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2023 Number 41, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 6856);
7. Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 244, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5587) as has been amended several times most recently by Law Number 6 of 2023 concerning Stipulation of Government Regulation in Lieu of Law Number 2 of 2022 concerning Job Creation into Law (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2023 Number 41, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 6856);
8. Law Number 8 of 2016 concerning Persons with Disabilities (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2016 Number 69, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5871);
9. Law Number 17 of 2023 concerning Health (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2023 Number 105, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 6887);
10. Presidential Regulation Number 25 of 2021 concerning Child-Friendly Regency/City Policy (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2021 Number 96);
11. Regional Regulation of Tanah Bumbu Regency Number 19 of 2016 concerning Establishment and Composition of Regional Apparatus (Regional Gazette of Tanah Bumbu Regency of 2016 Number 19) as has been amended several times most recently by Regional Regulation of Tanah Bumbu Regency Number 5 of 2021 concerning Second Amendment to Regional Regulation of Tanah Bumbu Regency Number 19 of 2016 concerning Establishment and Composition of Regional Apparatus (Regional Gazette of Tanah Bumbu Regency of 2021 Number 5);
12. Regional Regulation of Tanah Bumbu Regency Number 11 of 2021 concerning Implementation of Child-Friendly Regency (Regional Gazette of Tanah Bumbu Regency of 2021 Number 11, Supplement to the Regional Gazette of Tanah Bumbu Regency Number 126);

With Mutual Approval

REGIONAL HOUSE OF REPRESENTATIVES OF TANAH BUMBU REGENCY

and

REGENT OF TANAH BUMBU

DECIDES:

To Establish:
REGIONAL REGULATION CONCERNING IMPLEMENTATION OF WOMEN'S EMPOWERMENT AND CHILD PROTECTION.

Article 38

1. Special Protection for Children in conflict with the law as referred to in Article 36 letter b includes Children in conflict with the law and Children victims of criminal acts, which is the obligation and responsibility of the Regional Government, Police, Prosecutor's Office, Court, Parents, Family and Community.
2. Handling of Children in conflict with the legal process and implementation of Judge's decisions must continue to prioritize the Rights of the Child.
3. Special Protection for Children in conflict with the law as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be implemented through:
a. treatment of Children in a humane manner in accordance with the dignity and Rights of the Child;
b. provision of special accompanying officers for Children from an early stage;
c. provision of special facilities and infrastructure;
d. imposition of appropriate sanctions for the best interests of the Child;
e. continuous monitoring and recording of the development of Children in conflict with the law;
f. provision of guarantees to maintain relationships with parents or family; and
g. protection from identity disclosure through mass media and to avoid labeling.
4. In the case of a Child in conflict with the law, law enforcement officers shall not conduct justicia Child examination without the knowledge of parents or guardians, and shall be carried out by:
a. rehabilitation efforts through special institutions for the best interests of the Child;
b. protection efforts from identity disclosure through mass media and to avoid labeling;
c. provision of safety guarantees for victim witnesses and expert witnesses, both physical, mental and social; and
d. provision of accessibility to obtain information regarding case developments.

CHAPTER III

SCOPE

Article 6

The scope of regulation for the implementation of Women Empowerment and Child Protection includes:
a. Women Empowerment;
b. Women Protection;
c. Child Protection;
d. safe house;
e. Women Empowerment and Child Protection by Village government;
f. family quality;
g. cooperation;
h. awards;
i. Community participation;
j. financing;
k. prohibitions;
1. investigation provisions; and
m. criminal provisions.

